---
/**
 * Text Diff tool page.
 *
 * `client:load` for the same reason as the token counter: the island is the
 * page and it sits above the fold.
 */
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import TextDiff from '../../components/tools/TextDiff';
---

<ToolLayout slug="text-diff">
  <TextDiff client:load />

  <Fragment slot="about">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      Most online diff tools answer the wrong question. They tell you which lines differ, which is
      useful for code and close to useless for everything else. A reflowed paragraph reports as four
      deleted lines and four added ones, and a config file with one changed value reports the same
      way as one rewritten from scratch.
    </p>
    <p>
      The question usually being asked is <em>how much of this changed</em>, and a line count cannot
      answer it. Neither can most similarity scores, which run at the line level and score a
      one-word edit inside a long sentence as a total replacement.
    </p>

    <h2>The approach</h2>
    <p>
      Line alignment is Myers' greedy edit-graph walk, the algorithm behind <code>git diff</code>.
      It finds a minimal edit script rather than any script. That matters more than it sounds like
      it should: a naive longest-common-subsequence pass will happily pair line 4 with line 900
      because both read
      <code>}</code>, and produce a diff nobody can read.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then two refinements. Delete and insert runs are paired into modified rows and diffed again at
      the word level, so you see which words moved rather than which lines. And the similarity ratio
      is computed over characters: whole lines that survived count in full, and modified lines count
      only the words that survived. A one-word edit in a long paragraph reads as a small change,
      because it is one.
    </p>
    <p>
      Common prefix and suffix are stripped before any of that runs, and lines are interned to
      integers first, so two revisions of the same document diff in milliseconds even at a few
      thousand lines.
    </p>

    <h2>The other axis</h2>
    <p>
      Character overlap is not meaning, and the two fail in opposite directions. A paraphrase
      changes every word and no meaning: similarity near zero, meaning unchanged. A negation changes
      one word and inverts the claim: similarity near one, meaning reversed. Both are the same
      mistake, which is treating a lexical measure as a semantic one.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the semantic panel measures the second axis. The local engine builds TF-IDF vectors over
      word terms and character n-grams and compares them in the tab: real, fast, private, and
      lexical by construction, so it too will call a paraphrase different. The other engine sends
      both texts to this site&rsquo;s backend to be embedded by a model that does know that two
      different words can mean the same thing. That one is opt-in per click, and the button says
      what leaves the page before anything does.
    </p>
    <p>
      Either way the useful reading is the gap between the two figures. When they disagree by a lot,
      the panel says which way and what it usually means, because that disagreement is the finding.
      The segment table is where it becomes actionable: a headline score of 0.91 says the document
      is mostly the same, and the table says which paragraph is the one that is not.
    </p>

    <h2>Where it stops being trustworthy</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Beyond 2,000 edits the minimal alignment is abandoned and the divergent region is reported
        as one wholesale replacement. Counts and the similarity figure stay correct; the pairing
        inside that region does not. The tool says so when it happens.
      </li>
      <li>
        There is no move detection. A block relocated from the top of a file to the bottom shows as
        a deletion and an unrelated insertion, as it does in <code>git diff</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Ignoring whitespace or case changes what counts as equal, so the similarity figure moves
        with those switches. Compare like with like.
      </li>
      <li>
        Word-level highlighting only happens where the line diff paired two lines. A modified line
        with no counterpart is shown as a plain deletion.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Embedding models are weak on negation, measurably.</strong> Checked against the model
        this page uses: &ldquo;the cat sat on the mat&rdquo; against &ldquo;a feline was resting upon
        the rug&rdquo; scored 0.896, and against &ldquo;the cat did <em>not</em> sit on the mat&rdquo;
        it scored 0.920. The inverted claim scored <em>higher</em> than the paraphrase. Treat a high semantic
        score as &ldquo;same subject matter&rdquo;, never as &ldquo;same assertion&rdquo;, and read the
        lexical diff for the word that flipped.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Raw cosine is a bad scale for embeddings.</strong> Unrelated text sits around 0.65, not
        0, so the whole interesting range is squeezed into the top third. The interpretation bands account
        for that, and the angular metric spreads it out without changing the ranking. Reading cosine as
        a percentage is the most common way to misuse an embedding.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Segment alignment is greedy, and bounded.</strong> Best-first over the similarity matrix
        rather than an optimal assignment, which agrees with the optimal answer except on near-ties, where
        the pairing is ambiguous to a reader anyway. With the remote engine the number of segments per
        request is capped, and the panel says how many were covered rather than presenting a partial table
        as complete.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
